00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
Catherine snow says her mother-in-law  Charlene went to the Cape Breton  Regional Hospital Emergency Department  on December 30th snow says the  67-year-old hadn't been feeling well for  a few days and arrived at the ER with  intense jaw pain and flu-like symptoms  we're pretty sure she was triaged and  she waited for seven hours but it just  became too uncomfortable for her and she  decided that she would leave and try her  luck at the local  um the local Outpatient Clinic uh the  next morning but she never made it to  that clinic snow says her heart stopped  just an hour after she returned home  snow was clear sharing Charlene's story  is about getting answers and not placing  blame it would be reassuring for us if  we knew that everything was done that  could have been done  um and and if not we want to know why  veteran ER physician Robert Martel says  the community's trust in the system is  eroding especially after the recent  death of Allison holthoff who waited  seven hours for Care at an Amherst  Hospital people have to feel that they  can trust their system to care for them  when they need it Martel adds a start to  alleviating strain on emergency rooms  would be revisiting the triage system if  you were going to do triage  the way I believe it should be done the  front end of that triage should be a  physician uh with the ability to  discharge an individual order tests  Etc in a statement Health Minister  Michelle Thompson confirms an  investigation is underway into  Charlene's case and assures Nova  scotians any recommendations will be  acted on snow says she won't give up I'm  a stay-at-home mom I did not think I  would be sitting in this chair yesterday  morning advocating  provincially for better health care but  I'll do it for my mother-in-law and I'll  do it for my daughter who's missing her  man she's launched a new website  encouraging Nova scotians with similar  experiences to share their stories  Sky bride and Blom Global News hellofax
 

